The simulations are chosen based on the following reasons.

| Application                              | Where falling films occur                                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Evaporation and concentration**        | Dairy, fruit juice, sugar solutions, pharmaceuticals, caustic soda and solvent recovery. Falling-film evaporators provide high heat transfer with short residence time, useful for heat-sensitive fluids. ([DDPS Inc.][1]) |
| **Cooling and heat exchangers**          | Desalination, refrigeration, absorption chillers, reactor cooling and thermal management. Films may flow down vertical tubes, plates or over horizontal-tube banks. ([OSTI][2])                                            |
| **Gas absorption and chemical reactors** | Sulfonation, chlorination, oxidation, solvent absorption and wetted-wall reactors, where a thin film provides large gas–liquid interfacial area. ([ScienceDirect][3])                                                      |
| **Scrubbing and wastewater treatment**   | Gas scrubbers, cooling plates, humidification and contaminant removal. ([ScienceDirect][4])                                                                                                                                |
| **Coating and material processing**      | Paints, polymer solutions, photographic films, paper coatings and functional thin layers. Gravity-driven flow may occur before drying or solidification. ([arXiv][5])                                                      |
| **Fundamental hydrodynamics**            | Experiments on wave formation, instability, rivulets, dryout and heat/mass transfer enhancement. ([PMC][6])                                                                                                                |

[1]: https://www.ddpsinc.com/falling-film-evaporators?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Falling Film Evaporators | De Dietrich"
[2]: https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1838949?utm_source=chatgpt.com "1 Outline of the paper 1. Introduction 2. Falling film ..."
[3]: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1385894718323969?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Falling film reactor modelling for sulfonation reactions"
[4]: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0009250921007375?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Thickness distribution and fluctuation characteristics of ..."
[5]: https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01579?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Falling liquid films with blowing and suction"
[6]: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8068164/?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Experimental Validation of Falling Liquid Film Models - PMC"


| Fluid/application class             | Representative fluids                                   |              Film height (h_N) |     Mean velocity (u_N) |
| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | -----------------------------: | ----------------------: |
| **Water-based heat transfer**       | Water, dilute brine, aqueous refrigerant solutions      |            (0.05-1\ \text{mm}) |  (0.03-1.5\ \text{m/s}) |
| **Food and biochemical processing** | Milk, whey, juice, sugar solutions                      |             (0.1-2\ \text{mm}) |    (0.01-1\ \text{m/s}) |
| **Organic solvents/reactors**       | Ethanol, acetone, isopropanol, hydrocarbons             |            (0.03-1\ \text{mm}) |    (0.03-2\ \text{m/s}) |
| **Viscous laboratory liquids**      | Water–glycerol mixtures, silicone oils, mineral oils    |             (0.1-3\ \text{mm}) | (0.005-0.5\ \text{m/s}) |
| **Coating fluids**                  | Inks, paints and polymer solutions                      | (10\ \mu\text{m}-1\ \text{mm}) |    (0.01-2\ \text{m/s}) |
| **Scrubbing/wastewater films**      | Water, caustic solutions, contaminated aqueous mixtures |             (0.1-3\ \text{mm}) |    (0.02-2\ \text{m/s}) |

